    {
      "id": "s09",
      "type": "SET_LOGIC",
      "transition": "zoom",
      "background": "zoneA",
      "narration": "Say four things on the page could plausibly be what you want. Buttons, links, all sorts. And underscore takes two descriptions and keeps only what satisfies both — so it has to be a button, and it has to be titled Save. Save draft is a link, so it goes. Submit has no title. Cancel's title says Close. One survivor.",
      "data": {
        "setLogic": {
          "op": "and_",
          "opNote": "both must be true",
          "criteria": [
            "is a button",
            "titled Save"
          ],
          "countLabel": "matched",
          "verdict": "exactly one survives",
          "caption": "and_ narrows. it never widens.",
          "color": "blue",
          "atWord": 2.667,
          "candidates": [
            {
              "label": "Save",
              "sub": "button · title=Save",
              "title": "keep",
              "atWord": 15.5
            },
            {
              "label": "Save draft",
              "sub": "link · title=Save",
              "title": "drop",
              "atWord": 22.333
            },
            {
              "label": "Submit",
              "sub": "button · no title",
              "title": "drop",
              "atWord": 26.583
            },
            {
              "label": "Cancel",
              "sub": "button · title=Close",
              "title": "drop",
              "atWord": 30.833
            }
          ],
          "verdictAtWord": 35.083
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 635,
      "audio": "audio/pw-dojo-17-advanced-locators_long_s09.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s10",
      "type": "CODE_RUN",
      "transition": "push",
      "background": "zoneB",
      "narration": "In code that's and underscore, with the trailing underscore. Which looks odd, I know. But and and or are reserved words in Python, so Playwright had no choice. Or underscore is the mirror image — whichever one turns up, take it. That's the pattern for when a submit either opens a dialog or throws an error and you genuinely don't know which.",
      "data": {
        "codeRun": {
          "filename": "test_combine.py",
          "language": "py",
          "resultLabel": "what matches",
          "color": "blue",
          "atWord": 2.667,
          "caption": "the underscore is Python, not Playwright",
          "lines": [
            {
              "text": "page.get_by_role(\"button\").and_(",
              "detail": "both descriptions at once",
              "sub": "button AND titled Save",
              "label": "and_",
              "atWord": 10.083
            },
            {
              "text": "    page.get_by_title(\"Save\")).click()"
            },
            {
              "text": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "dialog = page.get_by_role(\"dialog\")"
            },
            {
              "text": "error = page.get_by_role(\"alert\")",
              "detail": "two things it could turn into",
              "sub": "a dialog, or an error",
              "label": "either",
              "atWord": 24.917
            },
            {
              "text": "expect(dialog.or_(error))",
              "detail": "whichever one shows up first",
              "sub": "you do not have to guess",
              "label": "or_",
              "atWord": 36.417
            },
            {
              "text": "    .to_be_visible()",
              "detail": "and assert on whichever landed",
              "sub": "no guessing required",
              "label": "assert",
              "atWord": 43
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 634,
      "audio": "audio/pw-dojo-17-advanced-locators_long_s10.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s11",
      "type": "SET_LOGIC",
      "transition": "zoom",
      "background": "zoneA",
      "narration": "Now a different shape of question. Not what is this thing, but what does it contain. Filter with has-not keeps the containers that are missing something — so, every product card that hasn't got a Sold out badge on it. Three of these six survive. Try writing that as a CSS selector sometime. Go on.",
      "data": {
        "setLogic": {
          "op": "filter(has_not=…)",
          "opNote": "keeps what lacks a child",
          "criteria": [
            "is a product card",
            "no Sold out badge"
          ],
          "countLabel": "still buyable",
          "verdict": "three of six survive",
          "caption": "a check about children, not attributes",
          "color": "green",
          "atWord": 2.5,
          "candidates": [
            {
              "label": "Blue mug",
              "sub": "card · in stock",
              "title": "keep",
              "atWord": 15.25
            },
            {
              "label": "Red mug",
              "sub": "card · Sold out",
              "title": "drop",
              "atWord": 17.5
            },
            {
              "label": "Coaster set",
              "sub": "card · in stock",
              "title": "keep",
              "atWord": 19.75
            },
            {
              "label": "Tea towel",
              "sub": "card · Sold out",
              "title": "drop",
              "atWord": 22.833
            },
            {
              "label": "Chopboard",
              "sub": "card · in stock",
              "title": "keep",
              "atWord": 25.083
            }
          ],
          "verdictAtWord": 28.083
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 517,
      "audio": "audio/pw-dojo-17-advanced-locators_long_s11.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s12",
      "type": "CODE_RUN",
      "transition": "push",
      "background": "zoneB",
      "narration": "Here it is. Grab every product card, then filter out any that contain the words Sold out, and click the first one left. Notice the argument is a locator, not a string — you're handing filter a whole description to look for inside each card. And has equals does the opposite, keeping only the ones that do contain it.",
      "data": {
        "codeRun": {
          "filename": "test_shop.py",
          "language": "py",
          "resultLabel": "what survives",
          "color": "green",
          "atWord": 2.583,
          "caption": "the filter argument is a locator",
          "lines": [
            {
              "text": "in_stock = page.get_by_test_id(",
              "detail": "start with every card",
              "sub": "six of them on the page",
              "label": "all",
              "atWord": 8
            },
            {
              "text": "    \"product-card\") \\"
            },
            {
              "text": "    .filter(has_not=page.get_by_text(",
              "detail": "drop any containing this",
              "sub": "a locator, not a string",
              "label": "has_not",
              "atWord": 20.333
            },
            {
              "text": "        \"Sold out\"))"
            },
            {
              "text": "in_stock.first.click()",
              "detail": "and take the first one left",
              "sub": "three were still buyable",
              "label": "click",
              "atWord": 32.75
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 570,
      "audio": "audio/pw-dojo-17-advanced-locators_long_s12.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
